## Proof

We determine the 4-torsion subgroup $E(\mathbb{Q})[4] = \{P \in E(\mathbb{Q}) : 4P = \mathcal{O}\}$ of the elliptic curve $E: y^2 = x^3 - 5$.

**Step 1: The 2-torsion over $\mathbb{Q}$ is trivial.**

A point $P = (x, y) \in E(\mathbb{Q})$ satisfies $2P = \mathcal{O}$ if and only if $P = -P$. Since the inverse of $(x, y)$ on $E$ is $(x, -y)$, the condition $P = -P$ forces $y = 0$. Substituting into the curve equation gives $x^3 - 5 = 0$, i.e., $x = \sqrt[3]{5}$. The polynomial $t^3 - 5$ is irreducible over $\mathbb{Q}$ (it has no rational root, since $\pm 1, \pm 5$ all fail), so $\sqrt[3]{5} \notin \mathbb{Q}$. Hence there is no non-trivial rational 2-torsion point:

$$E(\mathbb{Q})[2] = \{\mathcal{O}\}.$$

**Step 2: The 4-torsion is trivial.**

Consider the multiplication-by-2 map restricted to the 4-torsion:

$$[\times 2] : E(\mathbb{Q})[4] \longrightarrow E(\mathbb{Q})[2].$$

This is well-defined: if $4P = \mathcal{O}$, then $2(2P) = \mathcal{O}$, so $2P \in E(\mathbb{Q})[2]$. Its kernel is

$$\ker([\times 2]) = \{P \in E(\mathbb{Q})[4] : 2P = \mathcal{O}\} = E(\mathbb{Q})[2] = \{\mathcal{O}\}.$$

Thus $[\times 2]$ is injective on $E(\mathbb{Q})[4]$, and its image is contained in $E(\mathbb{Q})[2] = \{\mathcal{O}\}$. Therefore $[\times 2]$ maps every element of $E(\mathbb{Q})[4]$ to $\mathcal{O}$, which means

$$E(\mathbb{Q})[4] \subseteq \ker([\times 2]) = E(\mathbb{Q})[2] = \{\mathcal{O}\}.$$

Since $\mathcal{O} \in E(\mathbb{Q})[4]$ trivially, we conclude

$$E(\mathbb{Q})[4] = \{\mathcal{O}\}.$$

**Conclusion.** The only 4-torsion point of $E: y^2 = x^3 - 5$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ is the point at infinity $\mathcal{O}$. The 4-torsion subgroup is trivial.

